// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row localvec_ref_coerces_to_slice_admits (tier 2, `admits`).
// A PACKAGE-LOCAL `struct Vec<T>` COERCES TO `&[T]` BECAUSE OF ITS NAME.
//
// FOUND 2026-09-10c while pricing the five bare-name `Vec` intercepts in
// sema_expr.cpp. `tools/dlog`'s name_intercept over that TU reported EIGHT
// `Vec` sites, not five, and a per-site read of the three the handed-down list
// omitted led out of the file: the `&Vec<T>` -> `&[T]` coercion in
// `types_compatible` (src/compiler/sema.cpp, the `struct_name() == "Vec"`
// disjunct) admits ANY struct named `Vec`.
//
// The site's own comment says "Hardcoded to the stdlib Vec struct" — it is not
// hardcoded to anything but the bare NAME, and the same comment states the
// mechanism that makes this unsound: "Vec's `{ptr, len, cap}` layout has
// `{ptr, len}` (the slice fat-pointer) as a prefix, so at the LLVM level the
// pointer is reused verbatim — no runtime conversion needed". The struct below
// has NO pointer: the fat-pointer is read out of two i64 counters, 7 becomes
// the data pointer and 3 the length.
//
// Rust: a crate-local `struct Vec<T>` has no relationship to `std::vec::Vec`
// and no unsize coercion to `[T]`; `total(&v)` is a type error. Neither
// divergence registry carries a row or a clause for it (DIVERGENCES.md A1..A17
// searched by construct; docs/spec/* has no clause naming this coercion's
// operand identity).
//
// OBSERVED: compiles clean, links, and SEGVs — rc 139. The oracle is `admits`
// because the wrong behaviour is the COMPILE; the crash is the consequence.
//
// THE FIX SHAPE IS IN THE TREE: `is_stdlib_box` (sema_impl.hpp), bare name plus
// `pkg.empty() || pkg == "<owner>"`. Priced 2026-09-10c at the five quote/
// fn-macro sites as `vecqual`: 6406 fires, COST 0 over pass+fail(1478)+stdlib.
// This site is NOT one of the five and was not under that probe.
package localvec_ref_coerces_to_slice_admits;

pub struct Vec<T> {
    a: i64,
    b: i64,
}

fn total(s: &[i64]) -> i64 {
    let mut t: i64 = 0i64;
    for x in s {
        t = t + *x;
    }
    return t;
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let v: Vec<i64> = Vec { a: 7i64, b: 3i64 };
    let n: i64 = total(&v);
    if n != 0i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
